<html xmlns:o='urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office' xmlns:w='urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40'><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>Text To Word</title></head><body><p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Thanks everyone for joining. Today we'd like to understand your current recruitment process, challenges, goals, existing systems, and what you expect from a future solution.</p>
<p>Jennifer:</p>
<p>Sure. I'll start with some background.</p>
<p>We're operating across 35 countries and have approximately 28,000 employees. Recruitment has become increasingly difficult over the last few years. Growth has accelerated but our hiring processes haven't really evolved at the same pace.</p>
<p>Honestly, some of our processes still look the same as they did 8 years ago.</p>
<p>Robert:</p>
<p>That's actually true.</p>
<p>Some teams still maintain Excel sheets.</p>
<p>Sarah:</p>
<p>Not some teams. A lot of teams.</p>
<p>Robert:</p>
<p>Fair point.</p>
<p>Sarah:</p>
<p>And that's part of the problem. Everyone thinks they're following the same process but they aren't.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Can you elaborate?</p>
<p>Sarah:</p>
<p>For example, one recruiter might track candidates in Workday.</p>
<p>Another recruiter might export everything to Excel.</p>
<p>A hiring manager may keep separate notes in SharePoint.</p>
<p>Then someone asks for a status update and suddenly nobody agrees on where the latest information is.</p>
<p>Jennifer:</p>
<p>Exactly.</p>
<p>Last month we had an engineering position open for almost three months.</p>
<p>The recruiter believed there weren't enough qualified candidates.</p>
<p>The hiring manager kept complaining about delays.</p>
<p>Eventually we discovered there were more than 600 applications that hadn't been reviewed.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>What caused that?</p>
<p>Robert:</p>
<p>Volume.</p>
<p>We simply receive too many applications.</p>
<p>For some roles we receive 2,000 applications.</p>
<p>Recruiters manually review resumes.</p>
<p>There's no consistent screening process.</p>
<p>And honestly, sometimes recruiters miss strong candidates because they're overwhelmed.</p>
<p>David:</p>
<p>And every missed candidate potentially impacts the business.</p>
<p>Vacant positions cost money.</p>
<p>Delayed hiring costs money.</p>
<p>Recruiters spending hours on administrative work costs money.</p>
<p>Jennifer:</p>
<p>The frustrating part is that everybody knows the problems but we haven't had a unified solution.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>What systems are currently being used?</p>
<p>Michael:</p>
<p>Our official HR platform is Workday.</p>
<p>We use Microsoft Entra ID for authentication.</p>
<p>Microsoft Teams for collaboration.</p>
<p>Outlook for scheduling.</p>
<p>Power BI for reporting.</p>
<p>SharePoint for document management.</p>
<p>LinkedIn Recruiter for sourcing.</p>
<p>But there are also regional tools.</p>
<p>Some offices use local recruitment software.</p>
<p>Others maintain spreadsheets.</p>
<p>Sarah:</p>
<p>And recruiters don't always trust the data in Workday.</p>
<p>Michael:</p>
<p>That's more of a process issue than a technology issue.</p>
<p>Sarah:</p>
<p>Maybe, but it still affects operations.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>What business outcomes are you trying to achieve?</p>
<p>Jennifer:</p>
<p>Reduce recruiter workload.</p>
<p>Improve candidate experience.</p>
<p>Improve hiring quality.</p>
<p>Reduce time-to-hire.</p>
<p>Standardize recruitment globally.</p>
<p>Improve reporting.</p>
<p>David:</p>
<p>And reduce recruitment costs.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Any targets?</p>
<p>Jennifer:</p>
<p>Our current average hiring cycle is around 47 days.</p>
<p>We'd like to reduce that below 25 days.</p>
<p>Robert:</p>
<p>Recruiters spend too much time doing repetitive work.</p>
<p>Scheduling interviews.</p>
<p>Reviewing resumes.</p>
<p>Sending updates.</p>
<p>Tracking candidate status.</p>
<p>Gathering feedback.</p>
<p>Most of that should be automated.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Interesting.</p>
<p>What role do you see AI playing?</p>
<p>Robert:</p>
<p>A significant role eventually.</p>
<p>Resume parsing.</p>
<p>Candidate matching.</p>
<p>Candidate ranking.</p>
<p>Job description generation.</p>
<p>Interview question generation.</p>
<p>Candidate scoring.</p>
<p>Recruiter assistance.</p>
<p>Jennifer:</p>
<p>Let's be careful though.</p>
<p>AI should support recruiters.</p>
<p>It shouldn't make hiring decisions.</p>
<p>Robert:</p>
<p>Agreed.</p>
<p>We don't want automated hiring decisions.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Would those AI features be part of the MVP?</p>
<p>Jennifer:</p>
<p>No.</p>
<p>At least not all of them.</p>
<p>Resume parsing maybe.</p>
<p>Candidate matching perhaps.</p>
<p>But AI scoring and advanced analytics can come later.</p>
<p>Sarah:</p>
<p>Honestly I'd rather have a good workflow system first.</p>
<p>Robert:</p>
<p>Exactly.</p>
<p>Fix the process before adding advanced AI.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>So MVP first, advanced AI later?</p>
<p>Jennifer:</p>
<p>Yes.</p>
<p>Definitely.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>What should the MVP include?</p>
<p>Jennifer:</p>
<p>Job management.</p>
<p>Candidate management.</p>
<p>Resume upload.</p>
<p>Resume parsing.</p>
<p>Candidate search.</p>
<p>Recruiter dashboard.</p>
<p>Hiring manager dashboard.</p>
<p>Interview scheduling.</p>
<p>Notifications.</p>
<p>Analytics.</p>
<p>Reporting.</p>
<p>Single sign-on.</p>
<p>Audit logging.</p>
<p>Role-based access control.</p>
<p>Workday integration.</p>
<p>Teams integration.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Who are the primary users?</p>
<p>Sarah:</p>
<p>Recruiters.</p>
<p>Senior recruiters.</p>
<p>Hiring managers.</p>
<p>Department heads.</p>
<p>HR business partners.</p>
<p>Executives.</p>
<p>Candidates.</p>
<p>System administrators.</p>
<p>External interviewers.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Different permissions for each role?</p>
<p>Sarah:</p>
<p>Absolutely.</p>
<p>A candidate shouldn't see recruiter dashboards.</p>
<p>A recruiter shouldn't access administrative settings.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Makes sense.</p>
<p>What reporting capabilities are needed?</p>
<p>Jennifer:</p>
<p>Executive dashboards.</p>
<p>Time-to-hire.</p>
<p>Time-to-fill.</p>
<p>Recruiter productivity.</p>
<p>Candidate funnel analytics.</p>
<p>Offer acceptance rates.</p>
<p>Department performance.</p>
<p>Regional performance.</p>
<p>Hiring costs.</p>
<p>Diversity metrics.</p>
<p>Candidate satisfaction.</p>
<p>Sarah:</p>
<p>Recruiter workload would also be useful.</p>
<p>Robert:</p>
<p>And sourcing effectiveness.</p>
<p>Which channels produce the best candidates.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Any technology preferences?</p>
<p>Michael:</p>
<p>Yes.</p>
<p>Azure.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Mandatory?</p>
<p>Michael:</p>
<p>Not exactly mandatory.</p>
<p>Jennifer:</p>
<p>It's pretty close to mandatory.</p>
<p>Michael:</p>
<p>Technically there are alternatives.</p>
<p>Operationally we'd strongly prefer Azure.</p>
<p>Our security teams already understand Azure.</p>
<p>Our operations teams already support Azure.</p>
<p>Most of our internal platforms run there.</p>
<p>David:</p>
<p>Would AWS be cheaper?</p>
<p>Michael:</p>
<p>Possibly in some cases.</p>
<p>But migration and operational complexity would offset savings.</p>
<p>Jennifer:</p>
<p>Leadership would probably reject a recommendation that moved us away from Azure without a compelling reason.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Understood.</p>
<p>Azure is strongly preferred.</p>
<p>Michael:</p>
<p>Yes.</p>
<p>Very strongly preferred.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Any technologies you specifically want to avoid?</p>
<p>Michael:</p>
<p>AWS infrastructure if possible.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Let's discuss integrations.</p>
<p>Michael:</p>
<p>Mandatory:</p>
<p>Workday.</p>
<p>Microsoft Teams.</p>
<p>Outlook Calendar.</p>
<p>Microsoft Entra ID.</p>
<p>Future integrations:</p>
<p>LinkedIn Talent Solutions.</p>
<p>Indeed.</p>
<p>Glassdoor.</p>
<p>Assessment platforms.</p>
<p>Background verification vendors.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Any compliance requirements?</p>
<p>Michael:</p>
<p>GDPR is mandatory.</p>
<p>Audit logging.</p>
<p>Encryption at rest.</p>
<p>Encryption in transit.</p>
<p>Activity monitoring.</p>
<p>Role-based access control.</p>
<p>Multi-factor authentication.</p>
<p>Single sign-on.</p>
<p>Detailed audit trails.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Can you explain audit requirements?</p>
<p>Michael:</p>
<p>We need visibility into:</p>
<p>Who accessed candidate data.</p>
<p>When they accessed it.</p>
<p>What changes were made.</p>
<p>Who approved decisions.</p>
<p>Why actions occurred.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>What scale should the platform support?</p>
<p>Michael:</p>
<p>Today:</p>
<p>28,000 employees.</p>
<p>5,000 annual hires.</p>
<p>150,000 applications annually.</p>
<p>Future projections:</p>
<p>40,000 employees.</p>
<p>250,000 applications annually.</p>
<p>The architecture must support growth.</p>
<p>Sarah:</p>
<p>And growth isn't optional.</p>
<p>We're expanding aggressively.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Performance expectations?</p>
<p>Michael:</p>
<p>Search results under 2 seconds.</p>
<p>Resume processing under 10 seconds.</p>
<p>Dashboard loading under 5 seconds.</p>
<p>99.9% uptime minimum.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>What are the biggest frustrations for recruiters?</p>
<p>Robert:</p>
<p>Resume review.</p>
<p>Interview scheduling.</p>
<p>Candidate communication.</p>
<p>Feedback collection.</p>
<p>Reporting.</p>
<p>Status tracking.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>How are interviews scheduled today?</p>
<p>Sarah:</p>
<p>Mostly Outlook.</p>
<p>Email.</p>
<p>Calendar coordination.</p>
<p>Lots of manual work.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Could automation help significantly?</p>
<p>Sarah:</p>
<p>Absolutely.</p>
<p>Recruiters spend hours coordinating schedules.</p>
<p>Automating that would save hundreds of hours monthly.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>What should the candidate experience look like?</p>
<p>Jennifer:</p>
<p>Candidates should have a portal.</p>
<p>They should track applications.</p>
<p>Schedule interviews.</p>
<p>Upload documents.</p>
<p>Receive notifications.</p>
<p>Communicate with recruiters.</p>
<p>View application status.</p>
<p>Robert:</p>
<p>And not feel ignored.</p>
<p>That's important.</p>
<p>Candidates often complain about lack of communication.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Budget considerations?</p>
<p>David:</p>
<p>We have funding.</p>
<p>But we still want practical recommendations.</p>
<p>Reuse existing Microsoft investments where possible.</p>
<p>Reuse existing licenses where possible.</p>
<p>Avoid unnecessary complexity.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Timeline?</p>
<p>Jennifer:</p>
<p>Leadership wants an MVP in six months.</p>
<p>David:</p>
<p>That's ambitious.</p>
<p>Michael:</p>
<p>Possible if scope remains controlled.</p>
<p>Sarah:</p>
<p>And if requirements don't keep changing.</p>
<p>Everyone laughs.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>What would success look like one year after deployment?</p>
<p>Jennifer:</p>
<p>Faster hiring.</p>
<p>Better recruiter productivity.</p>
<p>Improved candidate experience.</p>
<p>Better reporting.</p>
<p>Improved hiring quality.</p>
<p>Global process standardization.</p>
<p>David:</p>
<p>Cost savings.</p>
<p>Don't forget cost savings.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>Anything else before we conclude?</p>
<p>Robert:</p>
<p>One thing.</p>
<p>I don't want a solution that looks impressive but nobody uses.</p>
<p>Adoption matters.</p>
<p>Jennifer:</p>
<p>That's actually one of my biggest concerns too.</p>
<p>If recruiters don't trust the system, they'll go back to spreadsheets.</p>
<p>And then we're right back where we started.</p>
<p>Consultant:</p>
<p>That's a very important point.</p>
<p>We'll include user adoption and change management considerations in our recommendations.</p>
<h2>&nbsp;</h2></body></html>